---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Selling Your Illinois Home Before Moving to Florida | AJ Tayfel"
  description="Planning to sell a home in Illinois before moving to Florida? Learn how to think through timing, preparation, closing coordination, and your next home purchase."
>
  <article>
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="container article-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Illinois → Florida Relocation</p>

        <h1>
          Selling Your Illinois Home Before Moving to Florida
        </h1>

        <p class="lede">
          For many Illinois homeowners, the hardest part of moving to Florida
          isn't choosing a destination. It's figuring out how to sell one home,
          buy another, and keep the timing from becoming unnecessarily
          complicated.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container article-layout">
      <main class="article-content">
        <p>
          If you're preparing to leave Illinois for Florida, your current home
          may be one of the most important pieces of the relocation plan.
          Decisions about when to list, when to begin shopping in Florida, and
          how much flexibility you have between closings can affect nearly every
          other part of the move.
        </p>

        <p>
          There isn't one sequence that works for every homeowner. The goal is
          to understand the moving pieces early enough that your Illinois sale
          and Florida plans can work together.
        </p>

        <h2>Start with the Illinois home</h2>

        <p>
          Before focusing too heavily on homes in Florida, it helps to
          understand what needs to happen with the property you're leaving.
          That usually means thinking through preparation, likely listing
          timing, your desired move date, and how much certainty you want before
          committing to another purchase.
        </p>

        <p>
          A practical first step is to identify what work, if any, should be
          completed before listing and how long that preparation may take.
          From there, you can build a more realistic relocation timeline.
        </p>

        <h2>Decide how much overlap you can comfortably manage</h2>

        <p>
          Some homeowners want their Illinois property sold before purchasing
          in Florida. Others may be comfortable owning both homes for a period
          of time. Some need the proceeds from the Illinois sale before they can
          complete the next purchase.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those situations can lead to very different strategies. Before
          choosing one, discuss financing questions with your lender and any
          legal or tax questions with the appropriate professionals.
        </p>

        <h2>Don't treat the two transactions as unrelated</h2>

        <p>
          An Illinois sale and a Florida purchase may involve different agents,
          lenders, attorneys or title professionals, inspectors, insurance
          providers, and closing processes.
        </p>

        <p>
          That makes communication important. Ideally, both sides understand
          the overall relocation timeline rather than working from two
          completely separate sets of assumptions.
        </p>

        <h2>Think about where you'll live between closings</h2>

        <p>
          Perfectly matching two closing dates isn't always realistic. Having
          a backup plan can reduce pressure if one transaction moves faster
          than the other.
        </p>

        <p>
          Depending on your circumstances, that could mean temporary housing,
          staying with family, arranging short-term accommodations, or
          discussing possession timing as part of your Illinois transaction
          when appropriate.
        </p>

        <h2>Begin researching Florida before you're ready to buy</h2>

        <p>
          You don't need to wait until your Illinois home is under contract to
          start learning about Southwest Florida. Early research can help you
          narrow communities, understand the types of properties available,
          and identify questions you'll want to ask a Florida real estate
          professional.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're considering Fort Myers, Cape Coral, Naples, Bonita Springs,
          Estero, or surrounding communities, see my
          <a href="/relocation/chicago-to-southwest-florida/">
            Chicago to Southwest Florida relocation guide
          </a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're still deciding which transaction should happen first,
          read
          <a href="/relocation/should-you-sell-illinois-house-before-buying-in-florida/">
            Should You Sell Your Illinois House Before Buying in Florida?
          </a>
          for a closer comparison of the three main sequencing options.
        </p>

        <h2>A simple way to think about the timeline</h2>

        <div class="steps">
          <section>
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>Plan</h3>
            <p>
              Clarify your Illinois property needs, desired move window, and
              destination goals.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>Prepare</h3>
            <p>
              Get the Illinois home ready for market while beginning more
              detailed Florida research.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>Coordinate</h3>
            <p>
              Keep the Illinois sale and Florida purchase timelines connected
              as each transaction develops.
            </p>
          </section>
        </div>

        <h2>How I can help with an Illinois-to-Florida move</h2>

        <p>
          I can help you plan and handle the Illinois real estate side of the
          relocation. When appropriate, I can also coordinate an introduction
          to a real estate professional who is licensed and experienced in the
          Florida market you're considering.
        </p>

        <p>
          That gives you a point of coordination instead of starting two
          completely unrelated real estate processes on your own.
        </p>

        <aside class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Planning note:</strong>
          This guide is general real estate information and is not legal, tax,
          lending, insurance, or financial advice. Specific questions in those
          areas should be reviewed with the appropriate licensed professional.
        </aside>
      </main>

      <aside class="article-cta">
        <div class="cta-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Planning a Move?</p>

          <h2>
            Start with your Illinois home and your relocation timeline.
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tell me where you are now, where you're considering moving, and
            what you're trying to coordinate.
          </p>

          <a
            class="button primary"
            href="/relocation/chicago-to-southwest-florida/#relocation-form-heading"
          >
            Start My Relocation Plan
          </a>

          <a
            class="secondary-link"
            href="/contact/"
          >
            Contact AJ
          </a>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-hero {
    padding: clamp(4rem, 8vw, 7rem) 1.25rem;
    background:
      linear-gradient(135deg, #0b3c5d, #145278);
    color: white;
  }

  .article-header {
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .article-header h1 {
    max-width: 850px;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.7rem, 6vw, 4.8rem);
    line-height: 1.04;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: .8rem;
    font-size: .82rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .11em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 760px;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,.88);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .article-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 340px;
    gap: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem);
    align-items: start;
    padding-top: 5rem;
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
  }

  .article-content {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .article-content p,
  .article-content li {
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .article-content h2 {
    margin-top: 3.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3vw, 2.35rem);
  }

  .article-content a {
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: .18em;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .steps section {
    padding: 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    border-radius: .75rem;
  }

  .steps span {
    color: var(--secondary);
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .steps h3 {
    margin: .5rem 0;
  }

  .steps p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    font-size: .94rem;
  }

  .article-cta {
    position: sticky;
    top: 7rem;
  }

  .cta-card {
    padding: 2rem;
    border-radius: 1rem;
    background: #f5f5f5;
  }

  .cta-card h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }

  .cta-card p:not(.eyebrow) {
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .cta-card .button {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .secondary-link {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    text-align: center;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .disclaimer {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding: 1.4rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--secondary);
    background: #f7f7f7;
    font-size: .9rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .article-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .article-cta {
      position: static;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .steps {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
